-- title:
--   Eq. (5): coherent remote-state preparation
-- statement:
--   A forward coherent bit has asymptotic remote-state-preparation rate one for the paper's restricted block task:
--
--   $$
--   [q\to qq]\ge1\text{ remote qubit}\qquad(a).
--   $$
--
--   Precisely, for every $\varepsilon,\delta>0$ and all sufficiently large resource counts $n$, there is an integer $m\ge0$ with $m^2\ge n(1-\delta)$ and a protocol using at most $n$ binary cobits and exactly zero externally supplied entanglement. It prepares any independently specified $m$ pure $m$-qubit states in Bob's lab with trace-distance error at most $\varepsilon$, uniformly over their choices. Alice knows the states; Bob's operations are fixed independently of their descriptions. Startup entanglement must be generated using counted cobit calls and can be recycled across the blocks. This does not assert preparation of one arbitrary $m^2$-qubit state.
-- source:
--   Coherent Communication of Classical Messages, arXiv:quant-ph/0307091v3, PDF p. 3, Eq. (5): coherent remote-state preparation

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Support21_P19

noncomputable section

